== Complete list of races ==

Thirty-three seats are up for election this year:
* Six Democrats are retiring.
* Fifteen Democrats are running for re-election.
* One Independent who caucuses with the Democrats is retiring.
* One Independent who caucuses with the Democrats is running for re-election.
* Three incumbent Republicans are retiring.
* Seven Republicans are seeking re-election

==== Arizona ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Arizona, 2012}}

Three-term incumbent and [[Assistant party leaders of the United States Senate|Senate Minority Whip]] Republican [[Jon Kyl]], who [[United States Senate election in Arizona, 2006|was re-elected in 2006]] with 53% of the vote, announced he would not seek a fourth term in 2012.<ref name=autogenerated3 /><ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.rollcall.com/news/-200909-1.html | title=Kyl Stays Tight-Lipped on Political Future | publisher=Roll Call | accessdate=February 14, 2011}}</ref>
Republican Representative [[Jeff Flake]]<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.www.foxnews.com/politics/2011/02/14/arizona-congressman-announces-senate-bid | title=Arizona Congressman Announces U.S. Senate Bid | publisher=Fox News | accessdate=February 14, 2011}}</ref> won the August 28th primary with 69.1% of the vote, against three contenders, including real estate investor [[Wil Cardon]]
<ref>http://www.azcentral.com/news/articles/2011/08/05/20110805kyl-senate-seat-candidate-mesa-investor05-ON.html</ref> who polled 21.2%.
On the Democratic side, former [[U.S. Surgeon General]] [[Richard Carmona]]<ref>http://www.rollcall.com/news/Richard_Carmona_Announces_Arizona_Senate_Bid-210227-1.html</ref> won the primary election, which was held August 28, 2012.
Both will face Libertarian Marc Victor, as well as two independent candidates, Ian Gilyeat and Michael F. Meyer

==== California ====
{{main|United States Senate election in California, 2012}}

Three and one-third term incumbent Democrat [[Dianne Feinstein]] was re-elected in 2006 with 59% of the vote, and is seeking reelection.<ref name="mccarty1"/> Feinstein finished first in the state's new [[blanket primary]],<ref name="CASOS">http://www.sos.ca.gov/elections/2012-elections/june-primary/pdf/notice-to-candidates-US-senate-2012.pdf</ref> in which the top two candidates advance to the general election regardless of party.

Elizabeth Emken came in second and will face Feinstein in the November election. The [[California Republican Party]] has endorsed [[Elizabeth Emken]].<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.highlandnews.net/articles/2012/03/12/opinion/columns/doc4f5e7236a9210332081965.txt | title=Emken Receives GOP Endorsement for US Senate}}</ref>

==== Connecticut ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Connecticut, 2012}}

Four term incumbent [[Joe Lieberman]], an [[Independent (politician)|independent]] who caucuses with the Democratic Party, decided not to seek re-election in 2012.<ref name="Lieberman won't run again">{{cite news | url=http://www.theday.com/article/20110118/NWS12/110119538/1017 | title=Lieberman won't run again &#124; News from southeastern Connecticut | publisher=The Day | accessdate=February 14, 2011}}</ref> Lieberman sat as a Democrat until 2006, when he was defeated by [[Ned Lamont]] in the Democratic primary; he then ran as an independent in the general election and he won. He has since caucused with the Democrats as an "Independent Democrat."

Former Connecticut Secretary of the State [[Susan Bysiewicz]]<ref name=autogenerated1/> and Congressman [[Chris Murphy (politician)|Chris Murphy]]<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2011/01/20/AR2011012002052.html | title=? | work=The Washington Post}}</ref>{{dead link | date=February 2011}} competed for the Democratic nomination. [[Linda McMahon]],<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.greenwichtime.com/local/article/McMahon-leaning-toward-2012-Senate-run-1020612.php | archiveurl=http://web.archive.org/web/20110711125322/http://www.greenwichtime.com/local/article/McMahon-leaning-toward-2012-Senate-run-1020612.php | archivedate=July 11, 2011 | title=McMahon 'leaning' toward 2012 Senate run | date=February 18, 2011 | accessdate=February 19, 2011 | work=[[Greenwich Time (D) (newspaper)|Greenwich Time]] | first=Neil | last=Vigdor}}</ref> the 2010 Republican Senate nominee, and former Congressmen [[Chris Shays]] competed for the GOP nomination.<ref>http://www.rollcall.com/news/shays_running_senate_connecticut-208086-1.html</ref> Murphy and McMahon won their respective primaries on August 14,<ref>{{cite news|title=McMahon, Murphy coast to Senate primary wins|url=http://www.stamfordadvocate.com/news/article/McMahon-Murphy-coast-to-Senate-primary-wins-3788564.php|accessdate=September 1, 2012|newspaper=StamfordAdvocate.com|date=August 15, 2012|author=Neil Vigdor|author2=Ken Dixon}}</ref>, and will face Libertarian Paul Passarelli.

==== Delaware ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Delaware, 2012}}

Two-term incumbent Democrat [[Tom Carper]] was reelected with 70% of the vote in 2006. Carper will seek re-election.<ref name=carper>{{cite news | url=http://www.politico.com/news/stories/1110/45375.html | title=Tom Carper to Harry Reid: Pick 2 chairmen | publisher=Politico | date=November 18, 2010 | first=David | last=Catanese}}</ref> His Republican opponent is businessman Kevin Wade.<ref>http://www.wdel.com/story.php?id=39890</ref>

==== Florida ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Florida, 2012}}

Two-term incumbent Democrat [[Bill Nelson]] was reelected with 60% of the vote in 2006 against controversial former [[Secretary of State of Florida]] [[Katherine Harris]]. Nelson is seeking re-election. [[Connie Mack IV]], Representative from {{ushr|Florida|14|}}, is the Republican nominee, having won the primary with 58.7% of the vote. The two [[Independent (politician)|Independents]] also seeking election in this race are [[Chris Borgia]], NPA and Bill Gaylor, NPA.

==== Hawaii ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Hawaii, 2012}}

Four-term incumbent Democrat [[Daniel Akaka]] was [[United States Senate election in Hawaii, 2006|re-elected in 2006]], but will not run for re-election in 2012.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.rollcall.com/news/Daniel-Akaka-Retiring-203790-1.html}}</ref> The primary elections were held on August 11, 2012.

Former Governor [[Linda Lingle]] won the Republican nomination. <ref>http://www.foxnews.com/politics/2012/08/12/former-hawaii-gov-linda-lingle-wins-gop-nomination-in-hawaii-for-us-senate/</ref> In the Democratic primary, Congresswoman [[Mazie Hirono]] defeated former Congressman [[Ed Case]], who challenged Akaka for the Democratic nomination in 2006. <ref>http://www.staradvertiser.com/s?action=login&f=y&id=165895716&id=165895716</ref> Lingle and Hirono previously faced each other in [[Hawaii gubernatorial election, 2002|Hawaii's 2002 gubernatorial election]], Lingle winning with 52% of the vote.

==== Indiana ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Indiana, 2012}}

Republican [[Richard Lugar]], who [[United States Senate election in Indiana, 2006|in 2006 won his sixth term]] without a Democratic opponent, was defeated in the 2012 primary election by [[Richard Mourdock]], who criticized Lugar for his willingness to vote with Democrats on key issues.<ref>http://usatoday30.usatoday.com/news/politics/story/2012-05-08/lugar-indiana-senate-mourdock/54844834/1</ref> According to Indiana law, Lugar cannot be a candidate in the election (R) (e.g. under a third party or as independent) after losing the primary.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://hotlineoncall.nationaljournal.com/archives/2011/03/could-lugar-run.php | title=Could Lugar Run As An Independent? | work=National Journal | date=March 10, 2011 | first=Christopher | last=Peleo-Lazar}}</ref> Mourdock won the primary 61% to 39%.<ref name="seattleweekly1"/> Congressman [[Joe Donnelly]] is the Democratic nominee.

==== Maine ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Maine, 2012}}

Three-term incumbent Republican [[Olympia Snowe]] was [[United States Senate election in Maine, 2006|re-elected in 2006]] with 73 percent of the vote, the largest margin of any incumbent senator in 2006 in a contested election.

On February 28, 2012, Snowe suddenly announced her retirement, saying she did not want to spend another six years in the Senate's hyper-partisan environment.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://politicalticker.blogs.cnn.com/2012/02/28/sen-snowe-to-retire | title= Maine senator will not seek re-election | work=CNN | date=February 28, 2012}}</ref> Popular former Governor [[Angus King]], an [[independent (politician)|independent]], has announced his candidacy and is considered the front-runner for the general election. King has not revealed which, if any, party he would caucus with.<ref>{{cite news | first=Scott | last=Thistle | title= King could be effective in the Senate without a caucus, former independent senator says | date=May 26, 2012 | work=Bangor Daily News | url=http://bangordailynews.com/2012/05/26/politics/king-could-be-effective-in-the-senate-without-a-caucus-former-independent-senator-says/}}</ref> Scott D'Amboise, considered a conservative, announced in February 2010 that he would run against Snowe in the Republican primary.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.asmainegoes.com/content/republican-scott-damboise-challenge-snowe-2012 | title=Republican Scott D'Amboise to Challenge Snowe in 2012 | publisher=As Maine Goes | date=February 8, 2010 | accessdate=February 14, 2011}}</ref> Tea Party candidate Andrew Ian Dodge is also announced a challenge to Snowe.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://voices.washingtonpost.com/thefix/republican-party/tea-partier-andrew-ian-dodge-l.html | title=Tea partier Andrew Ian Dodge launches campaign against Snowe | work=Washington Post | date=February 11, 2011 | accessdate=March 9, 2011}}</ref> After Snowe's announcement, other Republicans got into the race, including Secretary of State [[Charles Summers]], state Treasurer [[Bruce Poliquin]], and former state Senator [[Richard A. Bennett]].

Former Maine Secretary of State [[Matthew Dunlap]], a Democrat, announced in early November 2011 that he was running in the Democratic primary. Also in November, State Representative [[Jon Hinck]] announced he would run in the Democratic primary. Democratic State Senator [[Cynthia Dill]] announced her candidacy in January.

The primary election was held on June 12th. [[Cynthia Dill]] won the Democratic nomination with 44% of the vote and [[Charlie Summers]] took the Republican nomination with 32% of the vote. The two will face Independent former governor [[Angus King]], Andrew Ian Dodge on the Libertarian line, and two other independents: Ex-DEA Agent & USAF Veteran, Danny Dalton and Yarmouth Town Council Chair, Steve Woods.

==== Maryland ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Maryland, 2012}}

First-term incumbent Democrat [[Ben Cardin]] was [[United States Senate election in Maryland, 2006|elected in 2006]] with 54% of the vote against former [[Maryland]] Lt. Gov. [[Michael S. Steele]]. Cardin is running against [[Independent (politician)|Independent]] candidate [[Rob Sobhani|S. Rob Sobhani]],<ref name=SobhaniCBS>{{cite news|title=Another newcome enters U.S. Senate race against incumbent Senator Ben Cardin|url=http://baltimore.cbslocal.com/2012/09/18/another-newcomer-enters-u-s-senate-race-against-incumbent-sen-ben-cardin/}}</ref> Republican former [[U.S. Secret Service]] agent [[Daniel Bongino]],<ref name=CardinprimarySUN>{{cite news | last=Fritze | first=John | title=Ben Cardin wins Senate primary | url=http://www.baltimoresun.com/news/breaking/bs-md-congress-primary-2-20120403,0,5918965.story | accessdate=April 3, 2012 | newspaper=[[The Baltimore Sun]] | date=April 3, 2012}}</ref><ref name=BonginoWT>{{cite news | title=Ex-agent Bongino wins Republican Senate primary; will face Sen. Benjamin Cardin in November | url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/local/sen-benjamin-cardin-wins-democratic-primary/2012/04/03/gIQAh5dztS_story.html | accessdate=April 4, 2012 | date=April 4, 2012 | deadurl=yes}} {{Dead link | date=June 2012 | bot=RjwilmsiBot}}</ref> and Libertarian Dr. Dean Ahmad, a Palestinian refugee.

==== Massachusetts ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Massachusetts, 2012}}

Eight-term former Democrat [[Ted Kennedy]] [[United States Senate election in Massachusetts, 2006|was re-elected in 2006]], but died in August 2009. [[Paul G. Kirk]] was appointed as the interim senator by Governor [[Deval Patrick]], and served until Republican state legislator [[Scott Brown]] was elected in [[United States Senate special election in Massachusetts, 2010|a January 2010 special election]]. He is the first Republican senator to be elected in Massachusetts since 1972. Brown is running for election to a full term.<ref name=brown/> [[Harvard Law School]] professor [[Elizabeth Warren]] is the Democratic nominee.<ref name=Rizzuto>{{cite news | last=Rizzuto | first=Robert | title=Elizabeth Warren lands party endorsement with record 95 percent support at Massachusetts Democratic Convention | url=http://www.masslive.com/politics/index.ssf/2012/06/elizabeth_warren_lands_party_e.html | accessdate=June 2, 2012 | newspaper=The Republican | date=June 2, 2012}}</ref>

==== Michigan ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Michigan, 2012}}

Two-term incumbent Democrat [[Debbie Stabenow]] [[United States Senate election in Michigan, 2006|was re-elected in 2006]] with 57% of the vote to 41% for [[Oakland County, Michigan|Oakland County]] Sheriff and former [[Michigan Senate|State Senate Majority Leader]] [[Michael Bouchard]] after narrowly defeating Republican incumbent [[Spencer Abraham]] in 2000.

In the general election, Stabenow is running against Republican former Congressman [[Pete Hoekstra]] <ref>http://www.mlive.com/politics/index.ssf/2012/08/hoekstra_senate_michigan.html</ref> and Libertarian Scott Boman.

==== Minnesota ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Minnesota, 2012}}

One-term incumbent Democrat [[Amy Klobuchar]] was [[United States Senate election in Minnesota, 2006|elected in 2006]] with 58% of the vote. Senator Klobuchar's approval ratings, last reported at.,<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.surveyusa.com/client/PollReport.aspx?g=dc0da3bb-cd5a-4292-b619-0809ef9c07f2 | title=59% | publisher=Surveyusa.com | accessdate=May 13, 2011}}</ref> have steadily risen since her election in November 2006. Her quick response to the I-35 bridge collapse in her home city of Minneapolis and her travels around the state have solidified the senator's popularity with the citizens of Minnesota.

The Republican nominee will be state Representative [[Kurt Bills]], an economics teacher with libertarian-leaning views and a supporter of outgoing Congressman [[Ron Paul]].<ref>http://www.minnpost.com/politics-policy/2012/05/kurt-bills-wins-gop-endorsement-run-against-klobuchar</ref>

==== Mississippi ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Mississippi, 2012}}

One-term incumbent Republican [[Roger Wicker]] was appointed after former [[Party leaders of the United States Senate|Senate Majority Leader]] [[Trent Lott]] announced on November 26, 2007 that he was going to resign by the end of 2007.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/21973397 | title=Trent Lott announces his resignation - Politics | publisher=MSNBC | date=November 26, 2007 | accessdate=June 29, 2010}}</ref> Wicker defeated former Governor [[Ronnie Musgrove]] in the [[United States Senate special election in Mississippi, 2008|2008 special election]] and will be up for re-election in 2012.

On March 13, Wicker won the primary election with 89% of the vote, and will face [[Albert N. Gore Jr.]] of [[Starkville, Mississippi|Starkville]], who won the Democratic primary election with 57% of the vote, as well as Libertarian candidate Dr. Shaun Crowell, Constitution Party candidate Thomas Cramer, and Reform Party candidate Shawn O'Hara.

==== Missouri ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Missouri, 2012}}

Democrat [[Claire McCaskill]] was [[United States Senate election in Missouri, 2006|elected in 2006]] with 49.6% of the vote, defeating then-incumbent [[Jim Talent]]. McCaskill will seek re-election in 2012, so far with no prominent Democrat challenging her.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://pressreleases.kcstar.com/?q=node/45466| title=Senate Race 2012| date=November 23, 2010| publisher=pressreleases.kcstar.com| accessdate=December 1, 2010}}</ref> The primary election was held August 14.

Representative Todd Akin won the Republican nomination. Libertarian nominee Jonathan Dine will contest the election.

Republican primary contenders included Congressman [[Todd Akin]], former state Treasurer [[Sarah Steelman]],<ref>{{cite news | url=http://content.usatoday.com/communities/onpolitics/post/2011/05/todd-akin-missouri-senate-claire-mccaskill-/1| title=GOP Rep. Todd Akin to run for Senate in Missouri| date=May 17, 2011 | work=USA Today| accessdate=May 20, 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite news | url=http://sarahsteelman.com| title=Sarah Steelman for U.S. Senate 2012| date=November 29, 2010| publisher=sarahsteelman.com| accessdate=December 1, 2010}}</ref> and businessman John Brunner.<ref>{{cite web | first=David A. | last=Lieb | url=http://www.columbiamissourian.com/stories/2011/04/19/st-louis-businessman-might-challenge-mccaskill | title=St. Louis businessman might challenge McCaskill in Senate race | publisher=Columbia Missourian | accessdate=May 13, 2011}}</ref> Jim Talent declined to run again, and Attorney Ed Martin and former Missouri GOP Chair [[Ann Wagner]] are instead eyeing Missouri's 2nd Congressional District.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.stltoday.com/news/local/govt-and-politics/political-fix/article_796b7796-2a47-11e0-8531-00127992bc8b.html | title=Talent says no to Missouri Senate bid | date=January 27, 2011 | accessdate=January 27, 2011 | work=[[St. Louis Post-Dispatch]] | first=Bill | last=Lambrecht}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://thehill.com/blogs/ballot-box/house-races/159893-missouri-republican-ed-martin-shifts-to-2012-house-race- | title=Missouri Republican Ed Martin shifts to House race instead of Senate bid | date=May 9, 2011 | accessdate=May 9, 2011 | work=[[The Hill (D) (newspaper)|The Hill]] | first=Shane | last=D'Aprile}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.rollcall.com/news/Ann-Wagner-Missouri-2nd-district-exploratory-205146-1.html?pos=hln | title=Wagner Moves Toward Bid for Akin's Seat in Missouri : Roll Call Politics | publisher=Rollcall.com | date=April 26, 2011 | accessdate=May 13, 2011}}</ref>

==== Montana ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Montana, 2012}}

One-term incumbent Democrat [[Jon Tester]] was [[United States Senate election in Montana, 2006|elected in 2006]] by a 49.2% to 48.3% margin, narrowly defeating incumbent [[Conrad Burns]]. Tester will seek re-election,<ref name=tester>{{cite news | url=http://billingsgazette.com/news/state-and-regional/montana/article_30584e16-9227-11df-ad25-001cc4c03286.html | title=Horse Sense: Schweitzer not eyeing Senate seat | publisher=Billings Gazette | first=Charles S. | last=Johnson | date=July 17, 2010}}</ref> facing Republican [[United States House of Representatives|Congressman]] [[Denny Rehberg]] and Libertarian Dan Cox.

Rehberg<ref name="autogenerated7">{{cite web | url=http://billingsgazette.com/news/state-and-regional/montana/article_e0073c4e-7c2b-11df-b753-001cc4c002e0.html | title=It's official: Rehberg says he's taking on Tester | date=February 5, 2011 | accessdate=February 5, 2011 | work=[[Billings Gazette]] | first=Charles S. | last=Johnson}}</ref> won the June 5th Republican primary by 76.2% beating Dennis Teske, after former Republican Montana Governor and past RNC chairman [[Marc Racicot]]
<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.greatfallstribune.com/article/20101121/NEWS01/11210301 | title=Tester high on national GOP's hit list, observers say | date=November 21, 2010 | accessdate=November 21, 2010 | work=[[Great Falls Tribune]] | first=John S. | last=Adams}}</ref> dropped out.

==== Nebraska ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Nebraska, 2012}}

Two-term incumbent and former Governor Democrat [[Ben Nelson]] will not run for re-election in 2012.<ref name=nelson>{{cite news | url=http://www.politico.com/news/stories/1211/70879.html | title=Ben Nelson retiring from Senate | publisher=Politico | date=December 27, 2011}}</ref> Former [[Governor of Nebraska]] [[Bob Kerrey]], who held the Senate seat from 1989 to 2001, won the Democratic nomination.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/the-fix/post/bob-kerrey-changes-his-mind-will-run-for-senate-source-says/2012/02/27/gIQARWZ3dR_blog.html | title=Bob Kerrey changes his mind, will run for Senate, source says | date=February 27, 2012 | accessdate=February 27, 2012 | work=The Washington Post|first1=Aaron|last1=Blake|first2=Chris|last2=Cillizza}}</ref>

State Senator [[Deb Fischer]] defeated State Treasurer [[Don Stenberg]] and [[Nebraska Attorney General]] [[Jon Bruning]] for the Republican nomination.<ref name="bruning">{{cite news | url=http://www.omaha.com/article/20101105/NEWS01/101109834/559194 | title=Bruning 'ready to run' for Senate | date=November 5, 2010 | publisher=Omaha.com}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.omaha.com/article/20110628/NEWS01/706289903 | title=Fischer begins U.S. Senate bid | work=The Omaha World-Herald | date=June 28, 2011}}</ref> Independent Scott Rupert will also be on the ballot.

==== Nevada ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Nevada, 2012}}

Republican [[John Ensign]] [[United States Senate election in Nevada, 2006|won his second term in the Senate in 2006]] with 55% of the vote, defeating Democrat [[Jack Carter (R) (politician)|Jack Carter]]. In 2009, Ensign admitted to an affair with a campaign staffer.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.politico.com/news/stories/0609/23813.html | title=Admission clouds Nevada Sen. John Ensign's future – Manu Raju and John Bresnahan and Alexander Burns | publisher=Politico.Com | accessdate=June 29, 2010}}</ref> He initially announced that he was running for re-election,<ref name=ensign>{{cite news | url=http://www.lasvegassun.com/news/2009/jul/14/ensign-stay-senate-seek-re-election | title=Ensign to stay in Senate, seek re-election | date=July 14, 2009 | accessdate=July 14, 2009 | publisher=[[Las Vegas Sun]] | first=Lisa | last=Mascaro}}</ref> but in March 2011 announced that he would resign effective May 2011, and would not seek reelection.<ref>{{cite web | author=Justin89119 | url=http://www.lasvegassun.com/news/2011/apr/21/text-sen-john-ensigns-resignation-letter | title=Text of Sen. John Ensign's resignation letter – Thursday, April 21, 2011 &#124; 3:51&nbsp;pm | publisher=Las Vegas Sun | accessdate=May 13, 2011}}</ref> Governor [[Brian Sandoval]] appointed Republican Congressman [[Dean Heller]] to succeed him.

Heller is running for a full term in 2012.<ref>{{cite web | last=Catanese | first=David | url=http://www.politico.com/blogs/davidcatanese/0411/Sandoval_appoints_Heller.html?showall | title=Sandoval appoints Heller – David Catanese | publisher=Politico.Com | date=April 27, 2011 | accessdate=May 13, 2011}}</ref> Democratic Congresswoman [[Shelley Berkley]] is running.

==== New Jersey ====
{{main|United States Senate election in New Jersey, 2012}}

One-term incumbent Democrat [[Bob Menendez]] became the first Hispanic Senator to represent New Jersey in January 2006 when Former Senator [[Jon Corzine]] appointed him to the office after having resigned to become Governor, following his election to said office in November 2005. Menendez comfortably defeated Republican state Senator [[Thomas Kean, Jr.|Tom Kean, Jr.]], son of popular former Governor and [[9/11 Commission]] Chairman [[Thomas Kean|Tom Kean]] and was elected to a full term in [[United States Senate election in New Jersey, 2006|November 2006]].

The Republican Party nominee is state Senator [[Joseph M. Kyrillos]] of Monmouth County.<ref>http://www.nj.com/news/index.ssf/2012/06/kyrillos_wins_gop_nomination_t.html</ref> Independent candidates include small business owner [http://www.electdavidtosenate.com/ David Dranikoff], whose platform focuses mainly on the national debt and financial reform, alternative journalist Gavin Bard, a labor activist and [[anarcho-syndicalist]] who declared he will run as an independent based on major campaign tenets of opposing any regulation placed on the internet, abolishing CEOs, and convincing [[Sega]] to make the final game in the [[Shenmue]] series,<ref name="twitter1"/> as well as
Daryl Mikell Brooks, and Gwen Diakos. Rounding out the busy ballot are Libertarian Kenneth R. Kaplan, Socialist Greg Pason, Ken Wolski of the Green Party.

==== New Mexico ====
{{main|United States Senate election in New Mexico, 2012}}

Five-term incumbent Democrat [[Jeff Bingaman]] [[United States Senate election in New Mexico, 2006|was re-elected in 2006]] with 70.6% of the vote. Bingaman has decided to retire.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.marketwatch.com/story/democrat-sen-bingaman-to-retire-in-2012-ap-18 February 2011 | title=Democrat Sen. Bingaman to retire in 2012: AP | date=February 18, 2011 | accessdate=February 18, 2011 | work=[[marketwatch.com]] | first=Wallace | last=Witkowski }}</ref> Republicans running included businessman former U.S. Representative [[Heather Wilson]],<ref name=wilson>{{cite news | url=http://www.nmpolitics.net/index/2011/03/wilson-formally-enters-u-s-senate-race | title=Wilson formally enters U.S. Senate race | publisher=nmpolitics.net | date=March 7, 2011 |accessdate =March 7, 2011 }}</ref> and businessman [[Greg Sowards]]. Wilson won the Republican primary with 70% of the vote. The Independent American Party nominated Jon Barrie to run for the seat.

For the Democrats, Congressman [[Martin Heinrich]] defeated state Auditor [[Hector Balderas]] in the primary by a 59% to 41% margin.

The primaries were held on June 5.

==== New York ====
{{main|United States Senate election in New York, 2012}}

Two-term Democrat [[Hillary Rodham Clinton]] [[United States Senate election in New York, 2006|was re-elected in 2006]] with 67% of the vote. After narrowly losing the 2008 Democratic Presidential nomination to [[Barack Obama]], she resigned on January 21, 2009 when the [[United State Senate|Senate]] confirmed her as Secretary of State. On January 23, Governor [[David Paterson]] appointed Congresswoman [[Kirsten Gillibrand]] to the seat. Gillibrand won a [[United States Senate special election in New York, 2010|special election in 2010]] with 61.2% of the vote, and is seeking re-election.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.wivb.com/dpp/news/new_york/gillibrand-has-105m-in-reelection-bid | title=Gillibrand has $10.5M in reelection bid | date=July 16, 2012 | work=Wivb.com | publisher=LIN Television Corporation | accessdate=August 16, 2012}}</ref> Conservative attorney [[Wendy E. Long]] prevailed by a sizeable margin in the Republican primary.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://elections.nytimes.com/2012/primaries/congress/new-york | title=New York Congressional Primaries Election Results | accessdate=August 16, 2012}}</ref> Long has also been designated as the nominee for the [[Conservative Party of New York State]], and will appear on its ballot line as well as the Republican Party line in the general election.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.cpnys.org/posts/congratulations-wendy-long | title=Congratulations Wendy Long! | author=Shaun Marie | date=June 27, 2012 | accessdate=August 16, 2012}}</ref> Dr. Scott Noren of Ithaca NY is running as an Independent/Unaffiliated candidate against Senator Gillibrand and has a major student loan reform and anti-fracking platform.<ref>http://query.nictusa.com/cgi-bin/can_detail/S0NY00402/</ref> Chris Edes is the Libertarian Party candidate running on the issues of fiscal solvency, civil liberties and constitutional government. <ref>http://www.vote-for-chris.net/issues/</ref>

==== North Dakota ====
{{main|United States Senate election in North Dakota, 2012}}

Four-term incumbent Democrat [[Kent Conrad]] [[United States Senate election in North Dakota, 2006|was re-elected in 2006]] with 69% of the vote. Conrad has decided to not run for re-election in 2012.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.grandforksherald.com/event/article/id/190488 | title=Conrad will not run for re-election in 2012 | date=January 18, 2011 | accessdate=January 18, 2011 | work=[[Grand Forks Herald]] | first=Chuck | last=Haga}}</ref> U.S. Representative [[Rick Berg]]<ref>{{cite web | first=rebecca | last=Beitsch | url=http://www.bismarcktribune.com/news/local/govt-and-politics/article_a981a6a2-7be7-11e0-8412-001cc4c002e0.html | title=Rep. Rick Berg to announce Senate run | publisher=Bismarcktribune.com | date=May 11, 2011 | accessdate=June 27, 2011}}</ref> has announced his candidacy as a Republican. State Public Service Commissioner Brian Kalk initially announced his candidacy as well, but has since dropped it to run for the at large congressional seat held by Berg.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.rollcall.com/news/Kalk-Drops-Senate-Bid-for-House-205843-1.html?pos=htmbtxt | title=Kalk to Run for House Instead of Senate in North Dakota : Roll Call Politics | publisher=Rollcall.com | accessdate=June 27, 2011}}</ref> On November 8, 2011, former state Attorney General [[Heidi Heitkamp]] announced that she would be running for the Democratic nomination.<ref name="By Shira Toeplitz"/>

==== Ohio ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Ohio, 2012}}

One-term incumbent Democrat [[Sherrod Brown]] was [[United States Senate election in Ohio, 2006|elected in 2006]] with 56% of the vote, defeating then-incumbent [[Mike DeWine]], whose popularity suffered due to scandals involving former Republican Governor [[Bob Taft]] and ex-Congressman [[Bob Ney]]. Brown will seek re-election in 2012.

[[Ohio State Treasurer]] [[Josh Mandel]]<ref>{{cite web | last=Troyblade | first=Tom | url=http://www.toledoblade.com/Politics/2011/04/09/Ohio-treasurer-takes-step-toward-bid-for-U-S-Senate.print | title=Politics | publisher=Toledo Blade | accessdate=May 13, 2011}}</ref> won the March 6 Republican primary with 63% of the vote.<ref>http://www.politico.com/2012-election/map/#/Senate/2012/Primary/OH</ref>

==== Pennsylvania ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Pennsylvania, 2012}}

One-term incumbent Democrat [[Bob Casey, Jr.]], the son of popular former Governor [[Robert P. Casey|Bob Casey]], was [[United States Senate election in Pennsylvania, 2006|elected in 2006]] with 58.7% of the vote, defeating then-incumbent [[Rick Santorum]]. He was the first Democrat elected to a full term in the U.S. Senate from Pennsylvania since 1962. Casey will seek re-election.<ref name=casey>{{cite news | url=http://www.mcall.com/news/nationworld/pennsylvania/mc-dc-casey-2012-20101120,0,5867038.story | title=Mellow Casey has to up profile for re-election | publisher=The Morning Call | date=November 21, 2010}}</ref> He will face Republican candidate, Tom Smith, a businessman and former town supervisor, and Libertarian candidate, Rayburn Douglas Smith.

==== Rhode Island ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Rhode Island, 2012}}

One-term incumbent Democrat [[Sheldon Whitehouse]] was [[United States Senate election in Rhode Island, 2006|elected in 2006]] with 53.5% of the vote, defeating then-incumbent [[Lincoln Chafee]] by 7 percentage points. Whitehouse will seek reelection.

Businessman Barry Hinckley has announced that he would run against Whitehouse in the general election [http://www.golocalprov.com/news/who-is-barry-hinckley/].

==== Tennessee ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Tennessee, 2012}}

One-term incumbent Republican [[Bob Corker]] was [[United States Senate election in Tennessee, 2006|elected in 2006]] with 50.7% of the vote. He narrowly defeated then-Congressman [[Harold Ford, Jr.]]. Corker set up "the Bob Corker for Senate 2012 committee" on November 8, 2006, or one day after winning a six-year term.<ref>{{cite web | first=Michael | last=Davis | url=http://proquest.umi.com/pqdweb?did=1178614851&sid=1&Fmt=3&clientId=76566&RQT=309&VName=PQD | title=Corker sets up re-election panel 1 day after win. Action called necessary for 'full-time job' of fundraising. | work=[[Chattanooga Times Free Press]] | publisher=Tenn. Knight Ridder Tribune Business News. | date= December 12, 2006 }} (subscription)</ref> In November 2011, country music singer [[Hank Williams, Jr.]] expressed an interest in challenging Corker for the GOP nomination,<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.wsmv.com/politics/18033971/detail.html | title=Hank Williams Jr. Announces Senate Run – Politics News Story – WSMV Nashville | publisher=Wsmv.com | date=November 21, 2008 | accessdate=June 29, 2010}}</ref> but did not enter the primary. The primary election was held August 2, with Corker receiving 85% over four opponents
(Zach Poskevich, Fred Anderson, Mark Twain Clemens, and Brenda Lenard).

Corker's opponents are Mark E. Clayton, who won 30% in a seven-way Democratic primary, Libertarian candidate, Dr. Shaun Crowell, Kermit Steck (Constitution Party), Martin Pleasant (Green Party), and independents David Gatchell, James Higdon, Michael Joseph Long, Troy Stephen Scoggin, and Jacob Maurer.

==== Texas ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Texas, 2012}}

Three-term incumbent Republican [[Kay Bailey Hutchison]] was [[United States Senate election in Texas, 2006|re-elected in 2006]] with 62% of the vote despite a poor overall climate for Republicans, who lost control of both chambers of Congress that year. She announced in 2007 that she would not seek re-election, and she ran unsuccessfully for the Republican nomination for [[Texas Governor]] in 2009.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.chron.com/disp/story.mpl/front/5216932.html | title=Hutchison may leave U.S. Senate in 2009 &#124; Houston & Texas News &#124; Chron.com – Houston Chronicle | work=Houston Chronicle | date=October 16, 2007 | accessdate=June 29, 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/36114375/ns/politics-decision_2010 | title=Hutchison won't give up Texas Senate seat - Politics - Decision 2010 | publisher=MSNBC | date=March 31, 2010 | accessdate=June 29, 2010}}</ref> For the Republicans, former Texas Solicitor General [[Ted Cruz]] won in a runoff election against Texas Lt. Governor [[David Dewhurst]] 57% to 43%.[http://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/01/us/politics/cruz-defeats-dewhurst-for-gop-nomination-in-texas-senate-race.html]. Former [[SMU Mustangs]] running back and [[ESPN]] analyst [[Craig James (American football)|Craig James]], and former Dallas mayor [[Tom Leppert]] were also candidates.<ref>{{cite news | last=Feibel | first=Carolyn | url=http://www.chron.com/disp/story.mpl/front/6168001.html | title=White announces Senate campaign via Web video | work=Houston Chronicle | date=December 17, 2008 | accessdate=June 29, 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.dallasnews.com/sharedcontent/APStories/stories/D9543QVO0.html | title=?}}{{dead link | date=June 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.wfaa.com/news/local/Leppert-says-he-is-running-for-US-Senate-116930203.html | title=Leppert says he is running for U.S. Senate | date=February 25, 2011 | accessdate=February 25, 2011 | work=[[WFAA-TV]] | first=Brad | last=Watson}}</ref> In the May 29 primary, Dewhurst won 45% to Cruz's 34%.<ref>http://www.dallasnews.com/news/politics/headlines/20120530-dewhurst-cruz-launch-right-into-senate-runoff-battle.ece</ref>

Former Texas Representative [[Paul Sadler]] beat Grady Yarbrough in the Democratic runoff election [http://www.foxnews.com/politics/2012/07/31/texas-democrats-pick-ex-state-rep-sadler-for-us-senate/]. Also running as Democrats were Sean Hubbard, Addie Dainell Allen and Daniel Boone.

Dallas businessman John Jay Myers won the Libertarian primary with 78.9% of the vote, to become that party's candidate for Senate.

David Collins is running as the Green Party Candidate

==== Utah ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Utah, 2012}}

Six-term incumbent and former Senate Judiciary Committee Chairman Republican [[Orrin Hatch]] [[United States Senate election in Utah, 2006|was re-elected in 2006]] with 62% of the vote. He is running for re-election. Though Hatch remains popular with Utahns in general, he is viewed by many in the Utah Republican Party as too moderate, and many names were floated as potential challengers.<ref name=hatch>{{cite news | url=http://thehill.com/blogs/blog-briefing-room/news/97943-hatch-vows-to-run-for-re-election-in-2012 | title=Hatch vows to run for re-election in 2012 | publisher=The Hill}}</ref> The name most frequently mentioned was that of conservative Congressman [[Jason Chaffetz]], who had criticized Hatch, but Chaffetz announced on August 22, 2011, that he would not run against Hatch.<ref>[http://www.politico.com/blogs/davidcatanese/0811/Chaffetz_wont_challenge_Hatch.html]</ref> Also frequently mentioned is state Senator [[Dan Liljenquist]].<ref>{{cite web | url=http://washingtonexaminer.com/news/2011/08/rep-chaffetz-launch-statewide-campaign-tour | title=Rep. Chaffetz Launches Statewide Campaign Tour}}</ref> At the Utah Republican Party Caucus held on March 15, 2012, it was determined that state Senator Liljenquist would challenge US Senator Hatch in the June 26, primary.<ref>http://elections.mytimetovote.com/dates/utah.html</ref><ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.huffingtonpost.com/2012/04/21/orrin-hatch-primary_n_1442979.html | work=Huffington Post | title=GOP Senator Forced Into Primary Fight | date=April 21, 2012}}</ref> Hatch won the primary election with 66% of the vote.

Among Democrats, [[Scott Howell]], former state Senator and 2000 US Senate nominee, defeated [[Pete Ashdown]], CEO of [[XMission]] and 2006 nominee for U.S. Senate, in the Utah Democratic Party caucus to secure the nomination to challenge the Utah Republican Party nominee for the US Senate seat currently held by US Senator Orrin Hatch.<ref>http://www.deseretnews.com/article/865554451/Utah-Democratic-Senate-hopefuls-woo-delegate-support-votes-to-be-conducted-later-day.html</ref>

==== Vermont ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Vermont, 2012}}

One-term incumbent Independent [[Bernie Sanders]] is an [[Independent (politician)|independent]] Senator (and self-described [[democratic socialist]]) who caucuses with the Democrats and won election with 65.4% of the vote in 2006 after [[Jim Jeffords]], also an independent, retired.

[[John MacGovern]]<ref>http://www.vnews.com/03102012/8412568.htm</ref> and H. Brooke Paige<ref>http://vnews.com/primarysource/20120315/htm</ref> sought the Republican nomination, and MacGovern won the August 28 Republican primary.

==== Virginia ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Virginia, 2012}}

One-term incumbent Democrat [[Jim Webb]] was [[United States Senate election in Virginia, 2006|elected in 2006]] by a margin of 0.6%, narrowly defeating then-incumbent [[George Allen (U.S. politician)|George Allen]] in the biggest upset of the 2006 elections. Announcing that he did not want to spend his life in politics, Webb said he will not run for re-election.<ref>{{cite news | first=Catalina | last=Camia | title=Sen. Jim Webb won't run for re-election in 2010 | work=USA Today | date=February 9, 2011 | accessdate=February 9, 2011 | url=http://content.usatoday.com/communities/onpolitics/post/2011/02/jim-webb-senate-2012-/1}}</ref> On the Democratic Side, former [[Governor of Virginia]] and [[Democratic National Committee|DNC]] chairman [[Tim Kaine]] was unopposed for the nomination.

The Republican Party of Virginia decided that its nominee would be determined through a primary, as opposed to a state convention which had been used in more recent years. On January 24, 2011, [[George Allen (U.S. politician)|George Allen]] announced that he would seek the Republican nomination.<ref>{{cite news | title=Allen e-mails supporters; Webb reacts | publisher=Politico | url=http://www.politico.com/blogs/davidcatanese/0111/Allen_emails_supporters.html | first= David | last=Catanese | date=January 24, 2011 | accessdate =January 30, 2011}}</ref> [[Jamie Radtke]], head of the Virginia [[Tea Party Patriots]], has filed papers to run for the Republican nomination.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://politicalticker.blogs.cnn.com/2010/12/29/virginia-gets-a-tea-party-challenger | title=Virginia gets a Tea Party challenger | publisher=Politicalticker.blogs.cnn.com | date=December 29, 2010 | accessdate=February 14, 2011}}</ref> Also considering a run for the Republican nomination are state Del. [[Robert G. Marshall]] (R-Manassas) and [[Corey Stewart (politician)|Corey Stewart]], the chairman of the Prince William County Board of Supervisors. Marshall ran for the U.S. Senate in 2008, narrowly losing the Republican nomination at the state convention to former Governor [[Jim Gilmore]] by only 69 votes.

==== Washington ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Washington, 2012}}

Two-term incumbent Democrat [[Maria Cantwell]] [[United States Senate election in Washington, 2006|was re-elected in 2006]] with 57% of the vote over Republican businessman [[Mike McGavick]]. In her 2012 reelection, she will be challenged by Republican state Senator [[Michael Baumgartner]], a former U.S. State Department employee who represents a legislative district in the eastern part of the state.<ref name="seattleweekly1">http://blogs.seattleweekly.com/dailyweekly/2011/10/michael_baumgartner_a_republic.php</ref>

==== West Virginia ====
{{main|United States Senate election in West Virginia, 2012}}

Democratic Senator [[Robert Byrd]], the longest serving U.S. Senator in history, was [[United States Senate election in West Virginia, 2006|re-elected]] to his ninth term in 2006 but died in office on June 28, 2010. In the November 2010 [[United States Senate special election in West Virginia, 2010|special election]] to fill this seat, Democratic Governor [[Joe Manchin]] was elected, with 53.5% of the vote. Senator Manchin defeated Sheirl Fletcher in the May 15 Democratic primary election,
<ref>http://crow.herald-dispatch.com/elections/candidate.php?id=245</ref> to run for a full term.

[[John Raese]], the Republican nominee, was unchallenged in the primary election.<ref name="filing">[http://apps.sos.wv.gov/elections/candidate-search/search.aspx ''West Virginia Secretary of State'' "Candidate Search," Accessed January 29, 2012]</ref>

Bob Henry Baber is the candidate of the "The Mountain Party", which is the West Virginia affiliate of the Green Party.

==== Wisconsin ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Wisconsin, 2012}}

Four-term incumbent Democrat [[Herb Kohl]], owner of the [[National Basketball Association|NBA]]'s [[Milwaukee Bucks]] and member of the founding family of the [[Kohl's]] department store chain, announced he would not seek reelection in 2012.<ref name=kohl/> He was reelected in [[United States Senate election in Wisconsin, 2006|2006]] with 67% of the vote.

U.S. Representative [[Tammy Baldwin]] announced her intention to run for the seat on September 6.<ref>http://www.jsonline.com/news/statepolitics/129281483.html</ref> Other potential candidates for the Democrats include former Dane County Executive [[Kathleen Falk]], and Madison biotechnology executive Kevin Conroy.<ref>{{cite news | last=Kleefeld | first=Eric | url=http://tpmdc.talkingpointsmemo.com/2011/05/wis-dem-chair-russ-feingold-tammy-baldwin-could-run-for-senate-seat.php | title=Wis. Dem Chair: Russ Feingold, Tammy Baldwin Could Run For Senate Seat | publisher=TPMDC | date=May 13, 2011 | accessdate=May 13, 2011}}</ref> Both candidates declined to run and Baldwin became the only Democrat nominee.

On the Republican side, Business Mananger [[Eric Hovde]] Former Congressman [[Mark Neumann]], Speaker of the Wisconsin State Assembly [[Jeff Fitzgerald]], and former [[Governor of Wisconsin|Governor]] and former [[Secretary of Health and Human Services]], [[Tommy Thompson]] confirmed their candidacies for the seat.<ref>{{cite news | last=Wenzel | first=Patti | url=http://thirdcoastdigest.com/2011/05/senator-herb-kohl-to-retire-after-current-term | title=Senator Herb Kohl to retire after current term | publisher=ThirdCoast Digest | date=May 13, 2011 | accessdate=May 13, 2011}}</ref> The primary was held August 14. Thompson won the Republican nomination with 34% of the vote. Hovde came in second with 31%. Baldwin was unopposed in the Democratic primary and won.

The Libertarian candidate is Joseph Kexel, an IT consultant in Kenosha.

Nimrod Allen III, a former United States Marine, is running as an independent.

==== Wyoming ====
{{main|United States Senate election in Wyoming, 2012}}

One-term incumbent Republican Dr. [[John Barrasso]] was appointed to the Senate in 2007 after the death of Republican [[Craig L. Thomas]] and [[United States Senate special election in Wyoming, 2008|won a special election in 2008]] to complete Thomas's term. He will face Democrat Tim Chesnut, a member of the Albany County Board of Commissioners, and a rancher named Joel Otto who is running on the "Wyoming Country" line.

